<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Cologne</h1><p class="st-meta">1288 CE – 1794 CE (approx.) · capital Cologne</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest city of medieval Germany, which broke free of its archbishops at Worringen in 1288 and stayed a Catholic imperial city until the French came in 1794.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> grew from the Roman colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonia-claudia-ara-agrippinensium">Colonia Agrippina</a> and was ruled for centuries by its archbishops. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> fought them repeatedly, and the decisive victory came at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Battle of Worringen</a> in 1288, after which the archbishops could no longer reside in the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Frederick III</a> formally confirmed its imperial status in 1475.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After 1396 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verbundbrief">Verbundbrief</a> gave political power to 22 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>-like associations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> was the largest German city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, a leading Hanseatic member trading wine, cloth and metalwork. Its citizens founded a university in 1388, and the cathedral, begun in 1248 to house the shrine of the Three Kings, drew pilgrims. Cologne remained firmly Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French revolutionary troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> in 1794, and it was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1815 it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and became the main city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>. The cathedral was completed in 1880 and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-cologne.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Cologne" width="320" height="314"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DEU_Koeln_COA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1288 CE – 1794 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cologne</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ripuarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cologne, Köln</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1541&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-cologne" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Cologne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4838 411 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Cologne lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5158 667L5162 673M5158 667L5229 694M5158 667L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5162 673h0M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5158 667h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5158 667h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cologne. <a href="/atlas?year=1541&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-cologne">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
